            <fr:display-uri>4QZE</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/4QZE/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="The gradients we need for FlashAttention">The gradients we need for <fr:link href="/daoFlashAttentionFastMemoryEfficient2022/" title="FlashAttention: Fast and Memory-Efficient Exact Attention with IO-Awareness" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/daoFlashAttentionFastMemoryEfficient2022/" display-uri="daoFlashAttentionFastMemoryEfficient2022" type="local">FlashAttention</fr:link></fr:title>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter><html:p>Everything downstream is chain rule; the only step with any content is differentiating through the <fr:link href="/C00V/" title="Softmax" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/C00V/" display-uri="C00V" type="local">softmax</fr:link>. We take the gradients in dependency order, starting from <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[d\mathbf {O}]]></fr:tex> which arrives from the layer above.</html:p>
  <fr:tree show-metadata="false"><fr:frontmatter><fr:authors><fr:author><fr:link href="/kellenkanarios/" title="Kellen Kanarios" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/kellenkanarios/" display-uri="kellenkanarios" type="local">Kellen Kanarios</fr:link></fr:author></fr:authors><fr:date><fr:year>2026</fr:year><fr:month>8</fr:month><fr:day>3</fr:day></fr:date><fr:title text="d\mathbf {P} and d\mathbf {V} from \mathbf {O} = \mathbf {P}\mathbf {V}"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[d\mathbf {P}]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[d\mathbf {V}]]></fr:tex> from <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbf {O} = \mathbf {P}\mathbf {V}]]></fr:tex></fr:title></fr:frontmatter><fr:mainmatter>
<html:p>Both fall straight out of the product rule for matrices:</html:p>
<fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
  d\mathbf {P} = \frac {\partial  \phi }{\partial  \mathbf {O}} \frac {\partial  \mathbf {O}}{\partial  \mathbf {P}} = d\mathbf {O} \mathbf {V}^T,
  \qquad 
  d\mathbf {V} = \frac {\partial  \phi }{\partial  \mathbf {O}} \frac {\partial  \mathbf {O}}{\partial  \mathbf {V}} = \mathbf {P}^T d\mathbf {O}.
]]></fr:tex>
<html:p>The shapes are a useful sanity check. <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[d\mathbf {O}]]></fr:tex> has one entry per output, so it is <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[N \times  d]]></fr:tex>; <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbf {V}^T]]></fr:tex> is <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[d \times  N]]></fr:tex>; their product is <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[N \times  N]]></fr:tex>, matching <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbf {P}]]></fr:tex>. Likewise <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbf {P}^T d\mathbf {O}]]></fr:tex> is <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[N \times  d]]></fr:tex>, matching <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbf {V}]]></fr:tex>.</html:p>
<html:p>Componentwise, from <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbf {O}_{ik} = \sum _j \mathbf {P}_{ij} \mathbf {V}_{jk}]]></fr:tex>,</html:p>
<fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
  \begin {align*}
    d\mathbf {P}_{ij} &= \sum _{k} \frac {\partial  \phi }{\partial  \mathbf {O}_{ik}} \cdot  \frac {\partial  \mathbf {O}_{ik}}{\partial  \mathbf {P}_{ij}} = \sum _{k} d\mathbf {O}_{ik} \mathbf {V}_{jk}, \\
    d\mathbf {V}_{jk} &= \sum _{i} \frac {\partial  \phi }{\partial  \mathbf {O}_{ik}} \cdot  \frac {\partial  \mathbf {O}_{ik}}{\partial  \mathbf {V}_{jk}} = \sum _{i} d\mathbf {O}_{ik} \mathbf {P}_{ij} = \sum _{i} \frac {\exp  \left (\mathbf {Q}_{i} \cdot  \mathbf {K}_{j}\right )}{\mathbf {L}_{i}} d\mathbf {O}_{ik}
  .\end {align*}
]]></fr:tex>
<html:p>That last line is worth noting already: <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[d\mathbf {V}]]></fr:tex> never needs <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbf {P}]]></fr:tex> stored, only the ability to re-evaluate <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbf {P}_{ij}]]></fr:tex> from <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbf {Q}]]></fr:tex>, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbf {K}]]></fr:tex>, and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbf {L}]]></fr:tex>.</html:p>
</fr:mainmatter></fr:tree>

  <fr:tree show-metadata="false"><fr:frontmatter><fr:authors><fr:author><fr:link href="/kellenkanarios/" title="Kellen Kanarios" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/kellenkanarios/" display-uri="kellenkanarios" type="local">Kellen Kanarios</fr:link></fr:author></fr:authors><fr:date><fr:year>2026</fr:year><fr:month>8</fr:month><fr:day>3</fr:day></fr:date><fr:title text="d\mathbf {S} through the softmax"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[d\mathbf {S}]]></fr:tex> through the softmax</fr:title></fr:frontmatter><fr:mainmatter>
<html:p>Softmax acts row by row, so drop into the scalar case with <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbf {s}]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbf {p}]]></fr:tex> single rows. The <fr:link href="/C00V/" title="Softmax" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/C00V/" display-uri="C00V" type="local">softmax Jacobian</fr:link> is <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\partial  \mathbf {p}_i / \partial  \mathbf {s}_j = \mathbf {p}_i(\delta _{ij} - \mathbf {p}_j)]]></fr:tex>, giving</html:p>
<fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
  \begin {align*}
    d\mathbf {s}_j &= \sum _{i} d\mathbf {p}_i \cdot  \frac {\partial  \mathbf {p}_i}{\partial  \mathbf {s}_j} \\
                  &= \sum _{i} d\mathbf {p}_i \cdot  \mathbf {p}_i (\delta _{ij} - \mathbf {p}_{j}) \\
                  &= d\mathbf {p}_{j} \mathbf {p}_{j} - \mathbf {p}_{j}\sum _{i} d\mathbf {p}_{i} \mathbf {p}_{i}
  .\end {align*}
]]></fr:tex>
<html:p>As a row vector this is <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[d\mathbf {s} = \mathbf {p} \odot  (d\mathbf {p} - \mathbf {p} \cdot  d\mathbf {p})]]></fr:tex>, where the subtracted term is the <html:em>scalar</html:em> <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbf {p} \cdot  d\mathbf {p}]]></fr:tex> broadcast across the row. Collecting those scalars into a column <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbf {D}]]></fr:tex> with <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbf {D}_i = \mathbf {P}_i \cdot  d\mathbf {P}_i]]></fr:tex> recovers the matrix form</html:p>
<fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[d\mathbf {S} = \mathbf {P} \odot  (d\mathbf {P} - \mathbf {D}).]]></fr:tex>
<html:p><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbf {D}]]></fr:tex> is the piece that will cause trouble later: it is a dot product over an entire row of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbf {P}]]></fr:tex>, which is exactly the thing we refuse to materialize.</html:p>
</fr:mainmatter></fr:tree>

  <fr:tree show-metadata="false"><fr:frontmatter><fr:authors><fr:author><fr:link href="/kellenkanarios/" title="Kellen Kanarios" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/kellenkanarios/" display-uri="kellenkanarios" type="local">Kellen Kanarios</fr:link></fr:author></fr:authors><fr:date><fr:year>2026</fr:year><fr:month>8</fr:month><fr:day>3</fr:day></fr:date><fr:title text="d\mathbf {Q} and d\mathbf {K} from \mathbf {S} = \mathbf {Q}\mathbf {K}^T"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[d\mathbf {Q}]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[d\mathbf {K}]]></fr:tex> from <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbf {S} = \mathbf {Q}\mathbf {K}^T]]></fr:tex></fr:title></fr:frontmatter><fr:mainmatter>
<html:p>One more chain rule, and the sums run over opposite indices:</html:p>
<fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
  \begin {align*}
     d\mathbf {Q}_{i} &= \sum _{j} d\mathbf {S}_{ij} \mathbf {K}_{j} = \sum _{j} \mathbf {P}_{ij} \left (d\mathbf {P}_{ij} - \mathbf {D}_i\right ) \mathbf {K}_{j}, \\
     d\mathbf {K}_{j} &= \sum _{i} d\mathbf {S}_{ij} \mathbf {Q}_{i} = \sum _{i} \mathbf {P}_{ij} \left (d\mathbf {P}_{ij} - \mathbf {D}_i\right ) \mathbf {Q}_{i}
  .\end {align*}
]]></fr:tex>
</fr:mainmatter></fr:tree>

  <fr:tree show-metadata="false"><fr:frontmatter><fr:authors><fr:author><fr:link href="/kellenkanarios/" title="Kellen Kanarios" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/kellenkanarios/" display-uri="kellenkanarios" type="local">Kellen Kanarios</fr:link></fr:author></fr:authors><fr:date><fr:year>2026</fr:year><fr:month>8</fr:month><fr:day>3</fr:day></fr:date><fr:title text="Recomputing instead of storing">Recomputing instead of storing</fr:title></fr:frontmatter><fr:mainmatter>
<html:p>Every gradient above is expressed through <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbf {P}_{ij}]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[d\mathbf {P}_{ij}]]></fr:tex>, both <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[N \times  N]]></fr:tex>. Materializing either one is exactly the quadratic memory cost the forward pass went to such lengths to avoid, so the backward pass has to be able to produce those entries on demand.</html:p>

  
 <html:div class="jms-callout" style="
  margin: 1.1rem 0;
  padding: 0.1rem 0 0.1rem 0.9rem;
  border-left: 2px solid rgb(38, 97, 69);
  "><html:span style="font-weight: 500; color: rgb(38, 97, 69);">Insight.</html:span> 
Both are recoverable from the <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[N \times  d]]></fr:tex> tensors already resident in memory. The forward pass saved <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbf {L}]]></fr:tex>, so
<fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[\mathbf {P}_{ij} = \frac {\exp  \left (\mathbf {Q}_{i} \cdot  \mathbf {K}_{j}\right )}{\mathbf {L}_{i}},]]></fr:tex>
and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[d\mathbf {P}]]></fr:tex> is just an inner product of two rows,
<fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[d\mathbf {P}_{ij} = \sum _{k} d\mathbf {O}_{ik} \mathbf {V}_{jk} = d\mathbf {O}_{i} \cdot  \mathbf {V}_j.]]></fr:tex>
Neither ever requires an <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[N \times  N]]></fr:tex> intermediate.
</html:div>


<html:p>Substituting both into the previous section leaves <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[d\mathbf {Q}]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[d\mathbf {K}]]></fr:tex> in terms of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbf {Q}]]></fr:tex>, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbf {K}]]></fr:tex>, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbf {V}]]></fr:tex>, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[d\mathbf {O}]]></fr:tex>, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbf {L}]]></fr:tex>, and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbf {D}]]></fr:tex> alone:</html:p>
<fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
\begin {align*}
  d\mathbf {Q}_{i} &= \sum _{j} \frac {\exp  \left (\mathbf {Q}_{i} \cdot  \mathbf {K}_{j}\right )}{\mathbf {L}_{i}} \left (d\mathbf {O}_{i} \cdot  \mathbf {V}_j - \mathbf {D}_i\right ) \mathbf {K}_{j}, \\
  d\mathbf {K}_{j} &= \sum _{i} \frac {\exp  \left (\mathbf {Q}_{i} \cdot  \mathbf {K}_{j}\right )}{\mathbf {L}_{i}} \left (d\mathbf {O}_{i} \cdot  \mathbf {V}_j - \mathbf {D}_i\right ) \mathbf {Q}_{i}.
.\end {align*}
]]></fr:tex>
</fr:mainmatter></fr:tree>

  <fr:tree show-metadata="false"><fr:frontmatter><fr:authors><fr:author><fr:link href="/kellenkanarios/" title="Kellen Kanarios" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/kellenkanarios/" display-uri="kellenkanarios" type="local">Kellen Kanarios</fr:link></fr:author></fr:authors><fr:date><fr:year>2026</fr:year><fr:month>8</fr:month><fr:day>3</fr:day></fr:date><fr:title text="Getting \mathbf {D} for free">Getting <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbf {D}]]></fr:tex> for free</fr:title></fr:frontmatter><fr:mainmatter>
<html:p>One problem survives. <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbf {D}_i = \mathbf {P}_i \cdot  d\mathbf {P}_i]]></fr:tex> is a dot product over a full row, so computing it inside the streaming loop would mean a reduction over all <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[j]]></fr:tex> before any tile of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[d\mathbf {Q}_i]]></fr:tex> could be finished. This is the same global dependency the online softmax was designed to break.</html:p>
<html:p>The way out is that the row sum has already been computed once, in the forward pass. Expanding and pulling <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[d\mathbf {O}_i]]></fr:tex> out of the sum:</html:p>
<fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
  \begin {align*}
  \mathbf {D}_i &= \mathbf {P}_{i} \cdot  d\mathbf {P}_{i}  \\
  &= \sum _{j} \frac {\exp  \left (\mathbf {Q}_{i} \cdot  \mathbf {K}_{j}\right )}{\mathbf {L}_{i}} d\mathbf {P}_{ij} \\
  &= \sum _{j} \frac {\exp  \left (\mathbf {Q}_{i} \cdot  \mathbf {K}_{j}\right )}{\mathbf {L}_{i}} \left (d\mathbf {O}_{i} \cdot  \mathbf {V}_{j}\right ) \\
  &=  d\mathbf {O}_{i} \cdot  \sum _{j} \frac {\exp  \left (\mathbf {Q}_{i} \cdot  \mathbf {K}_{j}\right )}{\mathbf {L}_{i}} \mathbf {V}_{j} \\
  &= d\mathbf {O}_{i} \cdot  \mathbf {O}_{i}
  .\end {align*}
]]></fr:tex>
<html:p>The inner sum is precisely the definition of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbf {O}_i]]></fr:tex>. So <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbf {D}]]></fr:tex> is a single elementwise product of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[d\mathbf {O}]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbf {O}]]></fr:tex> summed along the head dimension — one <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[O(Nd)]]></fr:tex> pass over tensors we already have, done once before the backward loop begins.</html:p>
</fr:mainmatter></fr:tree>
</fr:mainmatter>
