            <fr:display-uri>7TMH</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/7TMH/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="Stabilizing Softmax in Attention">Stabilizing <fr:link href="/C00V/" title="Softmax" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/C00V/" display-uri="C00V" type="local">Softmax</fr:link> in <fr:link href="/004G/" title="Self-Attention" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/004G/" display-uri="004G" type="local">Attention</fr:link></fr:title>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>The <fr:link href="/C00V/" title="Softmax" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/C00V/" display-uri="C00V" type="local">softmax</fr:link> is ill-behaved due to the exponentials. In the transformer, we have two softmaxes: one at the end and one in the <fr:link href="/004G/" title="Self-Attention" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/004G/" display-uri="004G" type="local">attention</fr:link>.
<html:ul><html:li>For the first, note <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[\log  \sigma (\mathbf {x})_i = \log (x_i) - \log  \underbrace {\sum _{j=1}^{d} e^{x_j}}_{D(\mathbf {x})}]]></fr:tex>
   The only problem is the denominator term. The idea is to enforce the <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[D(\mathbf {x}) = 1]]></fr:tex> by regularizing via a penalty on <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\log  D(\mathbf {x})]]></fr:tex> ie.
<fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[\mathcal {L}_{\text {aux}} = 10^{-4} \log ^2(D(\mathbf {x}))]]></fr:tex>
With the <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\nabla  D(\mathbf {x})]]></fr:tex> should be <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[0]]></fr:tex> and we are effectively only considering the non-exponential term.
   </html:li>
   <html:li>For the <fr:link href="/004G/" title="Self-Attention" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/004G/" display-uri="004G" type="local">attention</fr:link>, they primarily operate on the <html:em>logits</html:em> prior to the softmax. One way is via <fr:link href="/LVV4/" title="Layer Normalization" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/LVV4/" display-uri="LVV4" type="local">layer norm</fr:link> before the <fr:link href="/C00V/" title="Softmax" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/C00V/" display-uri="C00V" type="local">softmax</fr:link>. Another is via <fr:link href="/39ZN/" title="Softcapping" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/39ZN/" display-uri="39ZN" type="local">Softcapping</fr:link></html:li></html:ul></html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors />
            <fr:date>
              <fr:year>2026</fr:year>
              <fr:month>7</fr:month>
              <fr:day>13</fr:day>
            </fr:date>
            <fr:uri>https://kellenkanarios.com/PMIC/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>PMIC</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/PMIC/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="Operator fusion is like ASIC caching"><fr:link href="/38CM/" title="Operator fusion" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/38CM/" display-uri="38CM" type="local">Operator fusion</fr:link> is like ASIC caching</fr:title>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>On a traditional CPU, the hardware manages <fr:link href="/M91V/" title="Caches" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/M91V/" display-uri="M91V" type="local">caching</fr:link> to ensure that data that is reused does not roundtrip through <fr:link href="/GWD5/" title="DRAM" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/GWD5/" display-uri="GWD5" type="local">DRAM</fr:link>. On an <fr:link href="/OGCRS/" title="ASIC accelerators" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/OGCRS/" display-uri="OGCRS" type="local">ASIC accelerators</fr:link>, we forego the complexity of caching to allow for more execution units. However, now if we have two operations i.e. elementwise add <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\to ]]></fr:tex> <fr:link href="/C00V/" title="Softmax" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/C00V/" display-uri="C00V" type="local">softmax</fr:link> this would</html:p>
            <html:ol><html:li>Load the input tensors from memory (HBM on <fr:link href="/NZZ7/" title="TPU Architecture" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/NZZ7/" display-uri="NZZ7" type="local">TPU</fr:link>).</html:li>
  <html:li>Perform the elementwise add.</html:li>
  <html:li>Store the output tensor to memory.</html:li>
  <html:li>Load the input tensors from memory.</html:li>
  <html:li>Perform the softmax.</html:li>
  <html:li>Store the output tensor to memory.</html:li></html:ol>
            <html:p>On a CPU, the input tensors would remain in the cache. However, this must be done explicitly on ASIC (or by the compiler in the case of Google / <fr:link href="/M74N/" title="XLA Compilation" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/M74N/" display-uri="M74N" type="local">XLA</fr:link>).</html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors>
              <fr:author>
                <fr:link href="/kellenkanarios/" title="Kellen Kanarios" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/kellenkanarios/" display-uri="kellenkanarios" type="local">Kellen Kanarios</fr:link>
              </fr:author>
            </fr:authors>
            <fr:date>
              <fr:year>2026</fr:year>
              <fr:month>5</fr:month>
              <fr:day>20</fr:day>
            </fr:date>
            <fr:uri>https://kellenkanarios.com/PI7N/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>PI7N</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/PI7N/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="Online Softmax">Online Softmax</fr:title>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter><html:p>This covers the math behind the online softmax algorithm required by <fr:link href="/R0PM/" title="Flash Attention (v1)" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/R0PM/" display-uri="R0PM" type="local">Flash Attention (v1)</fr:link>.</html:p>
  <fr:tree show-metadata="false"><fr:frontmatter><fr:authors><fr:author><fr:link href="/kellenkanarios/" title="Kellen Kanarios" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/kellenkanarios/" display-uri="kellenkanarios" type="local">Kellen Kanarios</fr:link></fr:author></fr:authors><fr:date><fr:year>2026</fr:year><fr:month>5</fr:month><fr:day>20</fr:day></fr:date><fr:title text="Safe softmax">Safe softmax</fr:title></fr:frontmatter><fr:mainmatter>
<html:p>The first thing we must consider is numerical precision. Namely, the <fr:link href="/C00V/" title="Softmax" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/C00V/" display-uri="C00V" type="local">original softmax</fr:link> operation is given by</html:p>
<fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[\mathrm {softmax}(\mathbf {x}) = \frac {\exp (\mathbf {x})}{\sum _{i=1}^n \exp (\mathbf {x}_i)}]]></fr:tex>
<html:p>However, when operating with <fr:link href="/DEQV/" title="Low Precision Data Types" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/DEQV/" display-uri="DEQV" type="local">low precision data types</fr:link> (i.e. float16) the maximum value is <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[65536]]></fr:tex>. Therefore, with <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\exp ]]></fr:tex> we can easily get numerically overflow.</html:p>
<html:p>To get around this, the online softmax algorithm adheres to the <html:em>safe softmax</html:em>. For safe softmax, we simply multiply the numerator and denominator by <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\exp (-m)]]></fr:tex>, where <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[m = \max  \{x_{1}, \ldots , x_n\}]]></fr:tex>. This gives</html:p>
<fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[\mathrm {softmax}(\mathbf {x}) = \frac {\exp (\mathbf {x} - m)}{\sum _{i=1}^n \exp (\mathbf {x}_i - m)}]]></fr:tex>
<html:p>Since <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[x_i - m \leq  0]]></fr:tex>, this ensures that each <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\exp (x_i - m)]]></fr:tex> is bounded in a safe range.</html:p>


  
 <html:div class="jms-callout" style="
  margin: 1.1rem 0;
  padding: 0.1rem 0 0.1rem 0.9rem;
  border-left: 2px solid rgb(121, 65, 66);
  "><html:span style="font-weight: 500; color: rgb(121, 65, 66);">Problem.</html:span> Note that computing the <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\max ]]></fr:tex> over <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbf {x}]]></fr:tex> means that we must have all of the logits in a row of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[QK^T]]></fr:tex> materialized in <fr:link href="/8GZN/" title="Shared memory (GPU)" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/8GZN/" display-uri="8GZN" type="local">SMEM</fr:link>, or first loop over all of them to compute the max before computing the normalization constant.</html:div>


</fr:mainmatter></fr:tree>

  <fr:tree show-metadata="false"><fr:frontmatter><fr:authors><fr:author><fr:link href="/kellenkanarios/" title="Kellen Kanarios" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/kellenkanarios/" display-uri="kellenkanarios" type="local">Kellen Kanarios</fr:link></fr:author></fr:authors><fr:date><fr:year>2026</fr:year><fr:month>5</fr:month><fr:day>20</fr:day></fr:date><fr:title text="Fusing the max and normalization">Fusing the max and normalization</fr:title></fr:frontmatter><fr:mainmatter>
<html:p>The online softmax algorithm fuses the max reduction into the normalization pass, so a single sweep over the row suffices to compute the normalization with the max. The key difficulty is that the denominator depends on <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n]]></fr:tex> <html:strong>for each</html:strong> <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[i]]></fr:tex> i.e.</html:p>
<fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[d = \sum _{i=1}^{n} \exp (x_i - m_n)]]></fr:tex>
<html:p>
Therefore, we must find a recursive relationship such that <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[d_{i+1} = f(d_i, m_i, x_{i+1})]]></fr:tex> for some function <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[f]]></fr:tex>. This would then allow us to locally compute <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[d_{i+1}]]></fr:tex> with only the local information of current <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[x_{i+1}]]></fr:tex> and the two running statistics <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[d_i]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[m_i]]></fr:tex>. This is exactly the kind of algorithm that is conducive to <fr:link href="/OGCRV/" title="Memory Tiling (GPU)" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/OGCRV/" display-uri="OGCRV" type="local">memory tiling</fr:link>.
</html:p>

  
 <html:div class="jms-callout" style="
  margin: 1.1rem 0;
  padding: 0.1rem 0 0.1rem 0.9rem;
  border-left: 2px solid rgb(115, 73, 35);
  "><html:span style="font-weight: 500; color: rgb(115, 73, 35);">Question.</html:span> What is <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[f(d_i, m_i, x_{i+1})]]></fr:tex>?</html:div>



  
  
 <html:div class="jms-callout" style="
  margin: 1.1rem 0;
  padding: 0.1rem 0 0.1rem 0.9rem;
  border-left: 2px solid rgb(115, 73, 35);
  "><html:span style="font-weight: 500; color: rgb(115, 73, 35);" /> 
  <html:details><html:summary><html:span class="foldout" style="font-weight: 500; color: rgb(115, 73, 35);">Answer.</html:span></html:summary>Consider <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[d_{i+1} = d_i \cdot  \exp (m_{i} - m_{i + 1}) + \exp (x_{i + 1} - m_{i + 1})]]></fr:tex>.</html:details>
</html:div>




  
  
 <html:div class="jms-callout" style="
  margin: 1.1rem 0;
  padding: 0.1rem 0 0.1rem 0.9rem;
  border-left: 2px solid rgb(109, 103, 101);
  "><html:span style="font-weight: 500; color: rgb(109, 103, 101);" /> 
  <html:details><html:summary><html:span class="foldout" style="font-weight: 500; color: rgb(109, 103, 101);">Proof.</html:span></html:summary>
  This can be seen by directly rewriting <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[d_{i + 1}]]></fr:tex>. 
  <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
    \begin {align*}
      d_{i + 1} &= \sum _{j = 1}^{i+1} \exp (x_j - m_{i + 1}) \\
      &= \left (\sum _{j = 1}^{i} \exp (x_j - m_{i+1})\right ) + \exp (x_{i+1} - m_{i+1}) \\
      &= \left (\sum _{j = 1}^{i} \exp (x_j - m_{i})\right )\exp (m_{i} - m_{i + 1}) + \exp (x_{i+1} - m_{i+1}) \\
      &= d_{i} \exp (m_{i} - m_{i + 1}) + \exp (x_{i+1} - m_{i+1})
    \end {align*}
  ]]></fr:tex>
</html:details>
</html:div>



<html:p>
The running denominator <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[d]]></fr:tex> is rescaled by <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[e^{m - m_{\text {new}}}]]></fr:tex> each time a new maximum is found, keeping it consistent with the safe-softmax shift.</html:p>
<html:div class="code-figure"><html:div class="code-filename">online_softmax.py</html:div><html:pre><html:code class="language-python">m = float("-inf")                    # running max of the logits
d = 0.0                              # running softmax denominator
for i in range(n):                   # one fused sweep for the stats
    m_new = max(m, x[i])
    d = d * exp(m - m_new) + exp(x[i] - m_new)
    m = m_new
p = [exp(x[i] - m) / d for i in range(n)]   # second pass: normalized probs</html:code></html:pre></html:div>

 <html:div class="jms-callout" style="
  margin: 1.1rem 0;
  padding: 0.1rem 0 0.1rem 0.9rem;
  border-left: 2px solid rgb(219, 216, 215);
  "><html:span style="font-weight: 500; color: rgb(109, 103, 101);">Remark.</html:span> 
Unfortunately, we cannot get around the second pass to compute the normalized output for <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbf {p}]]></fr:tex>. However, this is not the case in <fr:link href="/R0PM/" title="Flash Attention (v1)" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/R0PM/" display-uri="R0PM" type="local">Flash Attention (v1)</fr:link>.
</html:div>

</fr:mainmatter></fr:tree>
</fr:mainmatter>
